<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">French Grenada</h1><p class="st-meta">1649 CE – 1763 CE · capital Fort Royal</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">French colony on Grenada, founded after a brutal conquest of its Kalinago inhabitants, that grew sugar and cacao until Britain took it in the Seven Years' War.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacques-dyel-du-parquet">Jacques Dyel du Parquet</a>, governor of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/colony-of-martinique">Martinique</a>, bought rights to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/grenada">Grenada</a> and landed with settlers in 1649, founding a fort at the site of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-georges-grenada">St George's</a>. War with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalinago">Kalinago</a> followed; in 1651 the last defenders reportedly leapt from a cliff in the north, remembered as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/le-morne-des-sauteurs">Le Morne des Sauteurs</a>. Du Parquet sold the island to the Comte de Cérillac in 1657.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/grenada">Grenada</a> came under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-west-india-company">French West India Company</a> in 1664 and the crown in 1674, administered as a dependency of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/colony-of-martinique">Martinique</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-georges-grenada">Fort Royal</a> guarded a fine natural harbour. Settlers developed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cacao">cacao</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indigo">indigo</a> estates worked by enslaved Africans, and by the 1750s the colony had dozens of sugar mills. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> was the only permitted faith.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>British forces captured <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/grenada">Grenada</a> in 1762, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-paris-1763">Treaty of Paris</a> of 1763 ceded it to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a>, beginning the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/colony-of-grenada">Colony of Grenada</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> retook it in 1779 but returned it in 1783. French place names, Catholic parishes and a French-based <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/criollo-people">Creole</a> survive from this period.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/french-grenada.jpg" alt="French Grenada" width="320" height="192"><figcaption>Image: See file history below for details. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Grenada.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1649 CE – 1763 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Fort Royal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Jacques Dyel du Parquet</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>French</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/grenada">Grenada</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>La Grenade</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1706&amp;focus=french-grenada" aria-label="French Grenada on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2984 1727 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where French Grenada lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M3304 1983L5054 728M3304 1983L3303 1984"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5054 728h0M3303 1984h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3304 1983h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3304 1983h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1706&amp;focus=french-grenada">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
